From 9dd1333a15bfe4f01c791d32b634af705123595b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:12:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(build): MAINT-01 - a .dockerignore Docker was never going
 to read
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three things found by auditing what shipped rather than by reading it again.

1. THE .dockerignore WAS IN A PLACE DOCKER DOES NOT LOOK.

It sat at services/render-api/.dockerignore, next to the Dockerfile it was
written for, which reads as the obvious place and is a place Docker never
reads. Docker takes .dockerignore from the BUILD CONTEXT ROOT, and both images
in this repository are built with the repository root as their context — the
README's own deploy line is `fly deploy --dockerfile
services/render-api/Dockerfile`, run from the root, and docker-compose.yml says
`context: .`.

So it was silently dead. Measured on this checkout:

    context sent today                 673 MB   (564 MB of it .git)
    context after this change           85 MB

and that is a tree with no node_modules in it; on a working machine the figure
today is well over a gigabyte, all of it uploaded to the builder and baked into
a node:24-slim image on every deploy. Nothing failed, because nothing about a
fat image fails. It is just slow and large, which is exactly the kind of defect
that survives until somebody measures an image. The service has not been
deployed yet, so this was caught before it cost anything.

Moved to the root, where both builds read it, with the reason written at the
top so the next person does not "tidy" it back next to the Dockerfile. Two
paths added while it is finally live: /public, which vercel.json's buildCommand
fills with a second copy of every asset in apps/web/public, and /.vercel.

2. `pnpm --filter @ecowoods/render-api test` HAS NEVER RUN ANYTHING.

It answers "No projects matched the filters", because pnpm-workspace.yaml
covers apps/* and packages/* and this service lives under services/. Both of
its test files — seventeen assertions across the auth, the meter, the tile
loader, the plate and the PNG round trip — have never run automatically. The
command is in the README and in three of my own commit messages, and it was
never doing anything.

Fixed with a root script, `pnpm test:render-api`, which invokes node directly.
NOT by adding services/* to the workspace, which is the tidier-looking answer:
that adds an importer to pnpm-lock.yaml, this repository installs with
--frozen-lockfile, and a lockfile that does not match the workspace fails the
install for everyone in this tree including every other agent working in it. I
cannot run pnpm install in this environment to regenerate it and verify, and an
unverifiable lockfile edit is not worth a nicer command. The README now says
which command to use and why the other one cannot work.

3. fly.toml SET MAX_PIXELS AND NOT MAX_PLATE_PIXELS.

The plate endpoint's ceiling existed only as a default in the code. They are
separate costs — an uploaded photograph is decoded once, a plate is rendered
pixel by pixel, and 2.4M of the second is about a second and a half of CPU — so
both are declared where somebody looking up what this machine will do can find
them.

Nothing else moved. The audit came back clean on everything it could have
found: no stale references to the renderer internals renamed in CAM-02 and
DESIGN-01, no code left assuming a square tile, all six service PNG tiles
tracked, and the repo-root public/ correctly gitignored down to four
whitelisted files rather than carrying a second copy of the textures.

65 of 66 guards here; verify:css needs an install and passes on a machine that
has one. parse-scan clean on 642 files. Both service suites pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
